    "sourceRefs": [
      "old-help:rec-bg.htm",
      "era-changelog:era-iii-changelog.txt"
    ],
    "text": "Receiver BG Receiver BG (actions in battle) !!BG:XXXX; - in battle you can set/check/get the parameters of creature action. See also: triggers !?BG and ?!BR A$ Action type: $ = 0 – cancel action (stack theoretically can do this action, but it is impossible to do for some reason reason, for example, trying to escape from the shackles of war uses exactly this steps to cancel) 1 – hero casts spell 2 – walking 3 – protection 4 – run away from the battle 5 – pay off 6 – go and attack 7 – shoot 8 – wait 9 – wall attack (Catapult (specialty X1), Cyclops) 10 – creature casts spell (Fairytale dragon) 11 – First aid tent (treatment) 12 – no action (can be used as a waste of a unit’s turn) Comment (show) Attacking castle towers is considered normal shooting. You can check the shooting towers via MF:W. Note: when using BG:A flags (wait, walked, protect) are not set to the active stack. D$ Destination point (magic on a creature, movement or attack). See battlefield E$ Get the number of the unit to which the action of the stack is directed (-1 if the creature no) H$ Number hero – owner of the active stack (-1 = no hero) N$ Number of the current active stack (0..41). If used in a trigger !?BG1, returns the number of the unit to which the move will be transferred Q?$ Current walking side: left (0) or right (1). Only receive or check. S$ Number of the person being conjured spells Comment (show) If creature casts spell before an action (Sorcerers or Trolls (regeneration)), it goes before the trigger. If creature comes up to attack, then this option allows you to get the cell number where he will attack from. If creature fires, the value is -1. The number of the spell cast by creature is always -1. !!BG:A1; casts Hero !!BG:S?v1; v1 contains the number of this spell ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ !!BG:A10; casts creature !!BG:S?v1; the value in v1 will always be -1 An example of how you can force a creature with spell to conjure it: !!BG:A10 S-1 Dx1; At position x1 there should be an object on which magic is cast Notes on modern ERA versions !!BG:subcommands; In the table, $ denotes a read/write parameter, # an input, and ?$ an output. Individual commands define their identifiers and ranges. Action: 0 cancel, 1 hero spell, 2 move, 3 defend, 4 flee, 5 surrender, 6 move and attack, 7 shoot, 8 wait, 9 attack wall, 10 creature spell, 11 first-aid tent, 12 no action while spending the turn. Writing A does not automatically set the stack’s waited/defended/moved flags. Destination cell for movement, an attack, or a spell. Target stack; -1 if there is none. Hero owning the active stack; -1 if no hero exists. Active stack 0..41. In classic BG1 this can already be the next stack receiving control; use the separate OnBattleActionEnd event when exact action completion matters. Acting side: 0 attacker, 1 defender. Read only. Meaning depends on the action: the hero’s spell ID, the attack-origin cell for move-and-attack, or -1 for shooting and creature casting in the classic implementation. This receiver requires active battle context. Change an action before execution. Do not interpret S as a spell ID without checking A. Tower damage counts as shooting; MF provides additional context. Related reference BG · MF Related commands and tables Battle actions (BG) · Battle rounds (BR) · Physical damage (MF) · Battlefield hexes · Hero table · Spell table · ERM triggers and events",
