    "text": "Receiver MA Receiver MA (characteristics of creatures) !!MA:XXXX; - general change in characteristics creatures a certain type for all creatures on the map of that type. All commands can be set, checked, and retrieved. A#/$ Creature # has a $ attack B#/$ Creature # (caster) can cast his spell$ once per battle C#1/#2/$ Creature # worth: resource #2 in quantity $ Comment (show) Do not set the gold price (resource number - 6) to 0. If you set more than one additional. resource (0..5), will only be used first. If you want to change the second resource (in addition to gold) from first to second, set the previous resource to 0. !#MA:C1/2/3; - set the price to 3 ores (gold + ore) !#MA:C1/2/0 C1/0/5; - set the price to 5 wood and 0 ore (gold+wood) D#/$ Creature # has $ protection E#/$ Creature # has maximum damage $ F#/$ Creature # has a combat value of $ (used for AI) G#/$ Creature # has a growth rate of $ H#/$ Maximum quantity creature # on the map at the beginning of the game is $ I#/$ Creature # has a value of $ to AI L#/$ Creature # has level $(0..6) Comment: creatures considered higher than level 7 in the game also have $ = 6. M#/$ Creature # has minimum damage $ N#/$ Creature # has $shots O#/$ Creature # belongs lock $ (-1 = neutral) P#/$ Creature # has $health R#/$ Creature # has an additional increase of $ Comment: This refers to the increase during the construction of additional buildings. For example, Griffin Bastion gives +3 Griffins. Changing this meaning to Griffins, you will achieve a change in growth after building the Bastion. S#/$ Creature # has speed $ U#/$ Creature # can upgrade into creature $. $ = -2 – no improvement $ = -1 – use normal enhancement Comment (show) You can install your own set of improvements ;-) For example, like this: !#MA:U0/1 U1/2 U2/3 U3/4... It looks great and you can upgrade them all. If you set the circular improving two creatures, the player will be able to choose the better creature. You can use circular function (this is faster) to go through all creatures and installation of improvements for each (in the next creature). In a town, you can only improve what you can usually improve there. Yes, you you can install the Spearmen improvement in another creature, but you can improve them only in the Castle or Hill Fort. If you install the Halberdier upgrade in other creature, you can improve them only in the fort on the hill, since further upgrades for them are not allowed in towns. Note: using the command !!MA:U#/-2; causes any creature when installed will be upgraded into a Spearman (fixed in ERA). V#/$ Minimum quantity creature # at the beginning of the game the map is equal to $ X#/$ Install $ flag creature #. When working with flags, check their meanings very carefully (see. bit operations). Example (show) ; Give a flag to a creature !!IF:M^Crossbowmen now shoot twice^; !!MA:X2/?y1; [write all creature #2 flags to y1] !!VRy1: 32768; [add double attack flag] !!MA:X2/y1; [set new flag values for creature #2] ; Take the flag from the creature !!IF:M^Heavy Crossbowmen now fire once^; !!MA:X3/?y1; [write all creature #3 flags to y1] !!VRy1: 32768 -32768; [safe way: first give the flag, then take it away] !!MA:X3/y1; [set new flag values for creature #3] Related reference BM Related commands and tables Creature table · Resources · Town types · Repeated function calls (DO) · ERM debugging and compatibility · Creature bit flags · Variables and expressions (VR) <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:START --> ERA 3 changes These entries clarify or supersede historical behavior from the old ERM Help. - ERA 3.0.5, + — Added functions for SN:M arrays handling to \"Era Erm Framework\" mod. !?FU(Array_Join); ; Concatenates array items into single string, using passed glue string. Works with both numeric and string arrays. ; Example: &#91;'one'… <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:END -->",
