    "text": "Receiver MO Receiver MO (creature at position) !!MO#1/#2/#3:XXXX; – creature parameters in X Y L. Syntax extension 1: !!MO#1/#2/#3/#4:XXXX; – creature in X Y L coordinates, the fourth parameter is used to cancel the check for the existence of a creature at the beginning of the map. Syntax extension 2: !!MO$:XXXX; – creature in coordinates: v[$], v[$+1], v[$+2] Used to change the parameters of creatures in a certain position. Comment (show) Now the commands MO#1/#2/#3 and MO#1/#2/#3/1 have the same meaning. In both cases, the presence of the creature on the map is not necessary, but the format was retained for compatibility. Commands M, B and A can only be used for creatures modified in the editor (message, resource or artifact). You can't set the message to the creature, but you can change the aggressiveness, number, etc. If you try this then you will receive an error message, to avoid this, use option statuses UN:P904, or values OB:C to obtain a check number and process its value. Remember that you can adjust all the parameters of a creature if it is already placed on the map. For all creatures placed by the team UN:I, you can use any command. A$ Set/check/get artifact creatures in $ B#1/$2 Set/check/get qty. resource #1 in $2 (maybe negative) G$ Set/check/get number of creatures in $. Note: the maximum number of creatures in a squad on the map is 4095 (or 12 bits). With larger values, there is a possibility of data corruption regarding the squad’s aggression. M^Text^ Set the message. The length of the text cannot be longer than the original. Can contain any characters except ^ and ; M# Set text from global event number # M-1 Do not show the message specified in the editor Mz# Set/get message text into text variable M1/$ M1/-1 Set and get text parameters. You can use this command if the M command was used to set the message. M1/$ – number of the text variable in $ M1/-1 – cancel (restore default) message O# Set/check/get whether stack can increase # = 1 – quantity does not increase # < > 1 – quantity increases R$/# Set/check/get aggressivenessness $ – level of aggression 0 – pliable (will always join) 1..7 - friendly 1..10 – aggressive 4..10 – hostile 10 - bloodthirsty (will never join) # – phantom parameter (not used, but needed to differ from the old syntax), can take any values Example (show) Let's say we need to give the hero Gelare the ability to reduce the aggression of neutrals by 1 level... Here is a variant of such an implementation: !?OB54; !!OW:A-1/?y1; current hero !!FU&y1<>142:E; stop if not Gelare !!MO998:R?y2/1; find out the viciousness of creatures !!FU&y2=0:E; wait, creatures are already malleable !!VRv5249:Sy2; maintaining the original viciousness !!VRy3:S0; ; setting up new hostility... !!VRy3&y2>0/y2<4:S0; Pliable !!VRy3&y2>3/y2<8:S1; Friendly !!VRy3&y2>7/y2<10:S4; Aggressive !!VRy3&y2>9:S7; Hostile ; !!MO998:Ry3/1; change the viciousness !!IF:V540/1; set the “ability activation” flag !$OB54&540; Gelare “tapped” demand !!IF:V540/0; flag reset !!OB998:T?y1; !!FU&y1<>54:E; stop, if there is no creature already !!MO998:Rv5249/1; bring back the old viciousness Old syntax: MO:R0 (will join) or MO:R1 (will not join). U# Set/check/get whether creature can escape. # = 1 – cannot # < > 1 – maybe Related reference OB Related commands and tables General game operations (UN) · Map objects (OB) · General game operations (UN) · Artifact table · Resources <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:START --> ERA 3 changes These entries clarify or supersede historical behavior from the old ERM Help. - ERA 3.4.0, + — Improved ERM 2 compiler. It became possible to use named local variables and quick variables (f..t) as arrays subscripts. Syntax: (array&#91;index&#93;) or (array&#91;i&#93;). Index value is evaluated BEFORE current receiver, thus do no… - ERA 3.1.0, + — Improved UN:U command. It does not raise error if no more objects are found, but sets x-coordinate to -1 instead. Thus no premature objects counting is required. A new syntax with 6 parameters was added to allow using ar… - ERA 3.1.0, + — Added exported function FindNextObject (ObjType, ObjSubtype: integer; var x, y, z: integer; Direction: integer): integer; stdcall; It works the same as UN:U with fast search syntax, but does not raise error on no more ob… <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:END -->",
