    "text": "Receiver QU Receiver QU (task object) !!QU#1/#2/#3:XXXX; - Task object. Designed to change the attributes of the Seer's Hut or the Border Guard on the map in X Y L coordinates (#1/#2/#3). Syntax extension: !!QU#:XXXX; - Object-task by coordinates in variables v[#], v[#+1], v[#+2] Comments (show) When installing the Seer's Hut on the map (type 83) or Border Guard (type 9) using ERM, don't forget to call QU:I after the object is created. Otherwise, you will not be able to customize or interact with the object (most likely the game will crash). See also: receiver !!OB D$ Set/check/get the last day of the assignment at $. Comment: the object must have a job (checked using QU:Q), otherwise this command will cause an ERM error. I Initialize the object. M#/^text^ Set standard ERM string as message text. The line may contain variable mapping codes. # = 0 - message with offer # = 1 - progress message # = 2 - completion message Comment: the object must have a job (checked using QU:Q), otherwise this command will cause an ERM error. M#/$ Set/get the message text into a string variable. Extended syntax: M#/0 Delete message. Advanced syntax (for advanced users): M#/$ Set the text at the $ pointer as the message text or get a pointer to the message text. Comment: the object must have a job (checked using QU:Q), otherwise this command will cause an ERM error. N$ Set/check/get the number of the name of the seer (0..47). See file seerhut.txt See game resources for a complete list of names. Comment: There are only 48 names in the game. I wonder if more can be added... Q?$ Check/get job type in $: 0 - no task 1 - reach level 2 - achieve primary skills indicators 3 - kill the hero 4 - kill creature on the adventure map 5 - bring artifacts 6 - bring creatures 7 - bring resources 8 - visit by a certain hero 9 - be a player of a certain color Q0 Delete task Comment: you can't call without a task QU:D and QU:M. Q1/$ Set/check/gain experience level to be reached in $. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q2/$1/$2/$3/$4 Set/check/get Attack/pr/The Power of Magic/Knowledge to be achieved at $1/$2/$3/$4. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q3/$1/$2 Set/check/get hero number, to kill, $1 and playerswho completed the task, $2. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q4/$1/$2/$3/$4/$5 Set/check/get the coordinates of the creature to be killed to $1/$2/$3; number creatures, displayed in the quest log, $4; and player, who killed creature, at $5. The presence of a creature at the specified coordinates is not necessary. Please note that $4 may not correspond to the actual type of creature, this value is purely for beauty. If another creature appears at the specified coordinates and is killed, then the player who killed the previous creature will lose the right to complete the task. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q5 Clear the list of artifacts to bring Q5/#/$ Set/check/get qty. artifacts #, which need to be brought, in $. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q6 Clear the list of creatures to bring. Q6/#/$ Set/check/get qty. creatures #, which need to be brought, in $. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q7/#/$ Set/check/get qty. resources #, which need to be brought, in $. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q8/$ Set/check/get hero number, which should visit this object. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. Q9/$ Set/check/get player, which should visit this object. Comment: this will set the job type even when checking, because checking this for other job types is pointless. R$1/$2/$3 Set/check/get the type of reward in the Seer's Hut in $1 and reward values to $2 and $3. The reward can be of the following types: 0 - no reward 1 - $2 experience 2 - $2 spell points 3 - $2 morale 4 - $2 luck 5 - $3 units resource $2 6 - $3 points primary skill $2 7 - secondary skill $2 level $3 8 - artifact $2 9 - spell $2 10 - $3 creatures like $2 Comments: Please note, this command can only be called with all three parameters, even if only the first two are needed. Some values may contain garbage when checking rewards in preset huts (for example, the number of creatures). You will have to bring such values ​​to the correct size yourself. V$ Set/check/get player colourswho visited this facility. V#/$ Set/check/get, whether visited player # this object, in $: 0 - did not visit 1 - visited Related reference UN:I · H3Quests_RefreshData Related commands and tables ERM debugging and compatibility · Game object table · Game object table · Map objects (OB) · Hero table · Player colors · Creature table · Artifact table · Resources · Hero Skills · Secondary skills · Spell table",
