    "questions": [
      "Можно выбрать тип, тип/подтип либо точные координаты входа. Объект должен иметь входную клетку. Монстры, ресурсы и артефакты подходят; герои используют HE.",
      "Select an object type, type/subtype, or exact entrance coordinates. The object must have an entrance cell. Monsters, resources, and artifacts qualify; heroes use HE."
    ],
    "sourceRefs": [
      "old-help:tr-ob.htm",
      "era-source:erm.pas",
      "era-changelog:era-iii-changelog.txt"
    ],
    "text": "Trigger OB Trigger OB (objects) !?OB#1/#2/#3; - trigger when visiting an object at a certain point #1/#2/#3 (respectively, X Y L). !?OB#1/#2; - trigger when visiting any object of a certain type and subtype. !?OB#1; - trigger when visiting any object of a certain type. Works for any object with an \"input\" (yellow checkered in the editor). Note: This trigger includes creatures, artifacts and resources, but not heroes (for heroes use trigger !?HE) Object trigger options: !?OB#1/#2/#3; - will work when visiting an object at a certain point. !?OB#1/#2; - will work when visiting object type #1 / subtype #2 !?OB#1; - will work when visiting object type #1 If several variants of the trigger !?OB are used for one object, then trigger \"variant 2\" is triggered first, and then options \"1\" and \"3\". Options \"1\" and \"3\" are executed in the order they appear in the script. When trigger \"option 2\" is triggered, the object coordinates are written to v998, v999, v1000, as X Y L respectively. This way you can get the coordinates of any other visited objects without specifying them. Example: !?OB101; - any treasure chest on the map !!CH998:...; - change the options of the visited chest. You can use reference coordinates for all receivers that require precise coordinates using this pattern: !!XX@:…; , where XX - receiver name, @ = number v-variable storing the X coordinate (v[@]=X, v[@+1]=Y, v[@+2]=Level) Example: ... !!OB10:...; 10 is the number of the v-variable storing the X coordinate It is also possible through indirect links: ... !!OBv11:...; the value v11 is accepted as a reference, so v[v11]=X, v[v11+1]=Y, v[v11+2]=Level. Post-trigger OB !$OB#1/#2/#3; - post-trigger for an object at a certain point on the map. !$OB#1/#2; - post-trigger for any object type #1 / subtype #2. !$OB#1; - post-trigger for any object type #1. The principle of operation of the trigger is the same as that of a conventional trigger !?OB…; but this trigger is triggered after the standard properties of the object are executed. For example, post-trigger is used in a dwelling, triggering after the purchase of any creature. The post-trigger for the castle will trigger after the end of the battle if town was attacked. Example (show) !$OB16; ; after visiting the creature bank, variables v998/v999/v1000 contain the coordinates of the visited object !!CB998:G0/d/?y1 G1/d/?y2 G2/d/?y3 G3/d/?y4 G4/d/?y5 G5/d/?y6 G6/d/?y7; y1..y7 - number of guards in the units guarding the bank !!OB998&y1=0/y2=0/y3=0/ y4=0/y5=0/y6=0/y7=0:S; a ban on visiting the facility for all players if there are no guards (the bank is robbed) See also: receiver !!OB, object table When it fires Select an object type, type/subtype, or exact entrance coordinates. The object must have an entrance cell. Monsters, resources, and artifacts qualify; heroes use HE. Declaration !?OBtype; / !?OBtype/subtype; / !?OBx/y/level; Limitations and ordering The !$OB post-trigger runs after the default reaction. The three forms do not simply execute from general to specific: type/subtype runs first, followed by position and type handlers in script order. v998/v999/v1000 identify the visited object’s coordinates. Related reference Event catalog · Conditions · Functions Related commands and tables Map tiles (TR) · Visiting a hero (HE) · Game object table · Variables, strings and arrays · Map objects (OB) <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:START --> ERA 3 changes These entries clarify or supersede historical behavior from the old ERM Help. - ERA 3.0.0, + — ^....^ literal in any ERM command is interpolated and replaced by temporal z-variable index before receiver execution. This z-variable is released right after receiver is executed. Don't use string literals in WoG 3.58 h… <!-- ERA3-GENERATED:END -->",
